           running += x\n            count += seen[running - k]\n            seen[running] += 1\n        return count\n","approach":"**Prefix sums in a hash map.** Let `P[j]` be the sum of the first `j` elements. The subarray ending at index `j` and starting after index `i` sums to `P[j] - P[i]`, so we want the number of earlier prefixes equal to `P[j] - k`. Keep a map from prefix value to how many times it has occurred; at each step add `map[running - k]` to the answer and then record `running`.\n\n**Why it works:** every subarray corresponds to exactly one ordered pair of prefix indices `(i, j)` with `i < j`, and recording the current prefix *after* querying guarantees `i < j`. Seeding `{0: 1}` accounts for subarrays that start at index 0.\n\n**Common mistakes:** trying a sliding window (fails with negatives and zeros); recording before querying (counts the empty subarray when `k == 0`); forgetting the `{0: 1}` seed.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return the longest or shortest such subarray instead of the count? (store first or last index per prefix). Count subarrays whose sum is divisible by k? (same idea keyed on `prefix % k`). Two-dimensional version, counting submatrices summing to a target? (fix a row pair, collapse columns, reuse this).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(n)"},"similar":["two-sum","product-of-array-except-self","continuous-subarray-sum"],"number":11,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def subarraySum(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n        pass\n"}